WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.971
Welcome to Grade 5 English, Unit 4: Telling Timeless Tales.

2
00:00:04.051 --> 00:00:10.083
Unit 4 explores myths and legends, the timeless
tales families have retold for centuries.

3
00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:16.263
You will read an information text about the
two forms, the legend of the Whispering Dunes,

4
00:00:16.343 --> 00:00:19.971
and the three-part desert adventure "The Hasty Route".

5
00:00:20.051 --> 00:00:27.936
You will study twenty-five describing words, from hasty
and reluctant to justifiable and prosperous, in four groups.

6
00:00:28.016 --> 00:00:35.009
The language focus is the defining relative clause,
supported by present continuous for arranged plans,

7
00:00:35.089 --> 00:00:39.198
comparative adjectives, opposites and verb-noun collocations,

8
00:00:39.278 --> 00:00:43.867
and the unit closes with you writing
an original legend of your own.

9
00:00:43.947 --> 00:00:49.635
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:56.674
By the end of this unit, you will be able to
explain the difference between a myth and a legend.

11
00:00:56.754 --> 00:01:03.420
You will also identify the key features of
classic tales: oral tradition, timeless settings,

12
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.967
moral or explanatory purpose, and larger-than-life characters.

13
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:14.639
You will also use defining relative clauses
to add essential information to your sentences.

14
00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:17.352
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

15
00:01:17.432 --> 00:01:24.102
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: hasty,
reluctant, furious, anxious, grateful, irritable.

16
00:01:24.182 --> 00:01:29.462
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

17
00:01:29.542 --> 00:01:33.631
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

18
00:01:33.711 --> 00:01:41.326
The reading sequence includes Myths and Legends: What Makes
a Tale Timeless and The Legend of the Whispering Dunes.

19
00:01:41.406 --> 00:01:42.263
Read actively.

20
00:01:42.343 --> 00:01:46.880
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

21
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.229
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

22
00:01:51.309 --> 00:01:55.943
The first language focus is What
a Defining Relative Clause Does.

23
00:01:56.023 --> 00:02:02.470
A defining relative clause is a group of words
that tells the reader exactly which person,

24
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.935
place or thing the sentence means.

25
00:02:05.015 --> 00:02:09.722
Remove it and the sentence loses
the information the reader needs.

26
00:02:09.802 --> 00:02:17.627
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:17.707 --> 00:02:21.802
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Retell a Legend.

28
00:02:21.882 --> 00:02:26.544
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:26.624 --> 00:02:30.154
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:33.905
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:33.985 --> 00:02:35.657
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:35.737 --> 00:02:43.198
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:43.278 --> 00:02:46.855
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:46.935 --> 00:02:55.463
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
